---
description: Start work on the next feature in the queue using the harness flow.
argument-hint: "[feature-slug]"
allowed-tools: Read, Bash, Agent
---

Acting as the `leader`, begin work on the next feature. If a slug is given in `$ARGUMENTS`, use that feature; otherwise pick the top feature in the dependency-ordered queue (run `reins status` to see it) — `approved` features whose `dependsOn` are all `done` come first, then `pending` ones.

1. Run `<%= it.verifyCmd %>` to confirm a green starting state.
2. Read the feature's `lane` in `feature_list.json` (none recorded = `full`). For model and effort hints, run `npx reins route "<feature title>" --json` — but keep the recorded lane. Put `Lane: <lane>` on the first line of every subagent prompt.
<% if (it.isSdd) { %>3. **quick / chore — no spec.** Set it `in_progress` and launch `implementer`; for `chore`, follow with one `reviewer` pass. No discovery, no plan, no approval: the request was the approval.
4. **Approved fast-path (standard / full).** If the feature is `approved`, its plan or spec was already human-approved (typically during `/brainstorm`): do **not** re-open discovery and do **not** ask the human anything. Set it `in_progress`, launch `implementer`, then `reviewer` (and `security-reviewer` if the change touches auth, input, IO, secrets, or dependencies; `design-reviewer` if it touches UI, components, styles, or copy). Implement exactly this one feature, then stop.
5. **standard, `pending` — one plan, one gate.** Set it `analyzing`, read the relevant code (cheap explorers only for unfamiliar areas), copy `specs/_template/plan.md` to `specs/<feature>/plan.md` and fill it. Set it `spec_ready`, present the plan and its open questions in **one** message, and **stop**. When the human answers and approves (or runs `/approve-spec <feature>`), record the answers in the plan's **Resolution**, set it `approved`, and continue with the fast-path above.
6. **full, `pending` — discovery first.** Set it `analyzing` and investigate the codebase yourself — read the relevant files, and launch explorers for anything non-trivial (explorers are read-only and disposable: launch them cheap, e.g. `model: haiku`, low effort). Copy `specs/_template/discovery.md` to `specs/<feature>/discovery.md` and fill it: findings, affected areas, approaches, and the **open questions** a human must answer. Set the feature to `needs_clarification` and **stop** — present the open questions and wait. Never draft a spec from the bare title.
7. After the human resolves the questions (`/validate-discovery <feature>`), launch `spec_author` to write the spec grounded in the discovery; it sets the feature `spec_ready`. Stop for spec approval (`/approve-spec <feature>`), which marks it `approved` — then continue with the fast-path above.
<% } else { %>3. Set it `in_progress` and launch `implementer`. Then, by lane: `quick` — no reviewer, the gate is enough; `chore` — one `reviewer` pass (regressions only); `standard` / `full` — `reviewer` (and `security-reviewer` if the change touches auth, input, IO, secrets, or dependencies; `design-reviewer` if it touches UI, components, styles, or copy).
<% } %>
Remember: you orchestrate, subagents write their results to `progress/` (always in English), and exactly one feature is `in_progress` at a time.
